---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const internal = (segment) => `${base}/${segment.replace(/^\//, '')}`;

const areas = [
  [
    'Attention and selection',
    'When AI can produce more ideas than people can examine, how should scarce human attention be used? We study ways to choose what deserves deeper evaluation without quietly excluding unusual or uncertain possibilities.',
  ],
  [
    'Making knowledge useful',
    'Why do good ideas fail to become useful knowledge or practical capability? We study the gaps between finding information, understanding it, testing it and being able to act on it.',
  ],
  [
    'Representation and discovery',
    'How does the form in which information is presented change what people can notice, understand and discover? This includes experiments with visual and computational representations of complex data.',
  ],
  [
    'Economics of abundance',
    'What changes when information, software, analysis or other valuable goods become much cheaper to produce? We study the new bottlenecks, scarce complements and shifts in value that follow.',
  ],
];
---
<Layout
  title="Research"
  description="Research on attention, useful knowledge, representation and the economics of abundance in a world of increasingly capable AI."
>
  <header class="page-head">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">Research</p>
      <h1>What becomes scarce when knowledge becomes abundant?</h1>
      <p class="lede">Agalmic Research studies the gap between having more ideas and being able to do something worthwhile with them. The central questions concern attention, judgment, understanding, validation, representation and the practical use of knowledge.</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="section-number">Questions</span>
        <div class="section-copy">
          <h2>Four connected areas.</h2>
          <p>These are research questions, not separate departments or claims that a new field has already been established.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="grid">
        {areas.map(([title, copy], index) => (
          <article class="card">
            <span class="card-kicker">{String(index + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
            <h3>{title}</h3>
            <p>{copy}</p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Current study</p>
        <h2>How should scarce attention be allocated among too many plausible ideas?</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="large"><strong>Attention Allocation Under Cognitive Abundance</strong> is the main empirical study currently underway.</p>
        <p>The first historical study successfully reconstructed 1,815 usable ICLR paper candidates, but that sample was not large enough to answer the planned question with the required precision. The study stopped before looking at the outcome data.</p>
        <p>A larger follow-up is currently limited by the availability of historical data that can be collected without introducing selection bias. That is a useful result in itself: the present obstacle is trustworthy data, not more analysis code.</p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="button" href={internal('projects')}>See the project</a>
          <a class="button secondary" href={internal('results')}>See results and corrections</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">A real-world testbed</p>
        <h2>Representation can change what people notice.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p><a href="https://nemosyne.world">Nemosyne</a> is an independent project that can be used to test specific questions about representation and discovery.</p>
        <p>For example, different ways of arranging the same dataset may make patterns easier or harder to see. Those effects can be measured rather than assumed.</p>
        <p>Nemosyne does not belong to Agalmic Research, and software experiments cannot answer every economic or institutional question raised here.</p>
        <p><a href="https://nemosyne.world">Visit nemosyne.world ↗</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">For readers who want the machinery</p>
        <h2>Methods and research records remain open.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>The site also keeps the detailed methods, prior-art work, negative results and provenance records behind the research. They are useful for scrutiny and reuse, but they do not need to be the first thing every visitor sees.</p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="button secondary" href={internal('method')}>Methods</a>
          <a class="button secondary" href={internal('lineage')}>Prior work and lineage</a>
          <a class="button secondary" href={internal('provenance')}>Provenance</a>
          <a class="button secondary" href={internal('explore')}>Explore everything</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
